This module provides various useful functions for cats. It is aimed to be a proper replacement for most uses of [[Module:Cats]] ([[Module:Units/invoke]] being the exception).

== Usage ==

This module defines a "Cat" class that has various functions. The documentation is done with code annotations following the format of https://luals.github.io/wiki/definition-files/. This is the format that the Lua VSCode extension uses. You can copy this module to your local machine and get LSP support for the cat object. Take that Docbunto!

<syntaxhighlight lang="lua">
local Cat = require('Module:Cat2')
local data = mw.ext.bucket('cat')
	.select(unpack(Cat.required_fields))
	.where('id', 0)
	.run()
---@type Cat
local mohawk = Cat.from_bucket_data(data[1], 3)
assert(mohawk:name(), "Mohawk Cat")
</syntaxhighlight>

However, in most cases it's probably much easier to use [[Module:Cat query helper]] instead of manually querying the cat bucket. But for example, [[Module:DailyUnit]] is an exception to that.

== Sub-modules ==

A submodule can be loaded with <code>require</code> like so:

<syntaxhighlight lang="lua">
local Cat = require('Module:Cat2')
require('Module:Cat2/images').init(Cat)
require('Module:Cat2/string_functions').init(Cat)
</syntaxhighlight>

The reason it still uses <code>require</code> is so that [[Template:Dependency list]] is happy. Is the entire module system a bad design system? maybe.

=== Example ===

If you define a page called "Module:Cat2/omgnew", with this:

<syntaxhighlight lang="lua">
local omgnew = {}

---Convert cat names to JSON.
---@param cat Cat
---@return string
function omgnew.get_names_json(cat)
	return mw.text.jsonEncode(cat.names)
end

return {
	init = function(p)
		p.omgnew = omgnew
	end
}
</syntaxhighlight>

Then you can now use this submodule like:

<syntaxhighlight lang="lua">
local Cat = require('Module:Cat2')
require('Module:Cat2/omgnew').init(Cat)
-- ... (define `cat` as an instance of the `Cat` class.
mw.log(Cat.omgnew.get_names_json(cat))
</syntaxhighlight>

You ''can'' modify <code>p</code> however you want, but you should probably stick to the format that other submodules do. Especially, please stick to the format of returning return a table, with a function <code>init</code> that modifies the return package (<code>p</code>) of Cat2.

